      <section class="detail-block">
        <h2>Application Scenarios</h2>
        <div class="scenario-list">
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Pre-Solder Paste Printing PCB Cleaning</h3>
            <p>Solder paste printing is one of the most contamination-sensitive steps in SMT assembly. Dust particles on PCB pads prevent solder paste from adhering correctly, causing solder bridging, insufficient solder, or open joints after reflow. The SMT600 Series cleans both surfaces of PCBs inline before they reach the paste printer, ensuring pads are free of contamination at the most critical point in the SMT process.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Continuous High-Volume SMT Line Cleaning</h3>
            <p>SMT production lines run continuously at high throughput, and any cleaning step that requires manual intervention or causes line stoppages reduces overall equipment effectiveness (OEE). The SMT600 Series is designed as a fully automatic inline machine with industrial PLC control, matching the continuous conveyor speed of the SMT line and eliminating the cleaning step as a production constraint.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Bare Board Cleaning Before Paste Printing</h3>
            <p>The supported case is a bare board carrying loose, dry particles, cleaned before the solder paste printer. Particles on the copper or the solder mask at that point sit under the stencil and under everything printed on top of them. Positions later in the line are a different problem: the rollers must not contact printed paste, and an already-populated board brings component height, fragility and underside clearance into it &mdash; review those separately rather than assuming this machine covers them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block">
        <h2>Designed for SMT Line Integration</h2>
        <p>The SMT600 Series is built specifically for integration into SMT production lines, not as a standalone cleaning station. The industrial PLC control system communicates with line control systems, the automatic inline conveyor matches SMT line speeds, and the dual-sided upper and lower adhesive rollers clean both PCB surfaces simultaneously without a board flip. The quick-change window structure is built so the roller can be replaced without stripping the machine down. How long a changeover actually takes on your line is one of the things to establish at the trial rather than take from a datasheet. For compact line layouts where floor space is limited, compare with the <a href="/products/cleaning-machine/pcb-vertical-cleaner">PCB Vertical Cleaning System</a>.</p>
        <h3>What to send for an inline quotation</h3>
        <p>An inline cleaner is quoted against the line it goes into, not against a width. Send:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The board</strong> &mdash; length, width and thickness, the warp you actually see, any raised features, and whether anything is mounted on the underside.</li>
          <li><strong>Surface and contamination</strong> &mdash; solder mask or coating, and the particles you are trying to remove.</li>
          <li><strong>The conveyor</strong> &mdash; working height, direction of travel, which edge is supported, and the takt time the cleaner has to keep up with.</li>
          <li><strong>The interface</strong> &mdash; the actual handshake and I/O your line uses between machines, named from your own line documentation. We will match what is specified rather than assume a protocol.</li>
          <li><strong>Acceptance</strong> &mdash; how you will judge the result: particle count, board surface condition, and residual voltage, which are three separate checks.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>What to record during the trial itself is set out in <a href="/guides/what-is-web-cleaning#validation">how to test and maintain cleaning performance</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block faq-block">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Where in the SMT line should the SMT600 Series be installed?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Before the solder paste printer, on the bare board. That is the position this machine is built for, and the one to quote against. Any position after printing has to be reviewed on its own terms &mdash; the rollers must not contact printed paste, and a populated board raises component height, fragility and underside clearance, none of which this configuration addresses.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Does the SMT600 clean both sides of PCBs simultaneously?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Yes. The SMT600 Series uses dual upper and lower adhesive rollers that clean both PCB surfaces in a single pass as the board travels through the inline conveyor. That removes the board flip and the second pass a single-sided machine needs.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How does the PLC control system integrate with the SMT line?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">The industrial PLC communicates with the line's central control system for conveyor speed matching, start/stop synchronization, and alarm management. Contact us with your line control system specifications for integration requirements.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What PCB sizes does the SMT600 Series support?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">PCB size compatibility is specified during machine configuration. Provide your minimum and maximum board dimensions and conveyor width requirements at the quotation stage for a correctly configured machine.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How often does the adhesive roller need to be replaced?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Roller replacement frequency depends on production volume, PCB surface condition, and the level of contamination being removed. The quick-change window structure is designed for fast roller access, minimizing the time taken for scheduled roller changes. Typical replacement intervals are provided based on your production specification.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Is the SMT600 Series suitable for both leaded and lead-free SMT processes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Yes. The adhesive roller removes loose surface particles from a bare board, and that does not change between a leaded and a lead-free line. It is a dry process, so it introduces no water or chemistry of its own. It is not a flux-residue or ionic-contamination clean, and it does not replace one &mdash; those need an aqueous or solvent process.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

